  "generated_text": "To determine the number of different complementary three-card sets, we need to analyze the conditions given for each card:\n\n1. **Shape Condition:**\n   - There are 3 shapes: circle, square, and triangle.\n   - Each shape can be chosen independently, so there are \\(3\\) choices for each card.\n\n2. **Color Condition:**\n   - There are 3 colors: red, blue, and green.\n   - Each color can be chosen independently, so there are \\(3\\) choices for each card.\n\n3. **Shade Condition:**\n   - There are 3 shades: light, medium, and dark.\n   - Each shade can be chosen independently, so there are \\(3\\) choices for each card.\n\nTo find the total number of different complementary three-card sets, we need to consider the number of choices for each card and multiply them together:\n\n\\[\n3 \\text{ (choices for shape)} \\times 3 \\text{ (choices for color)} \\times 3 \\text{ (choices for shade)} = 3^3 = 27\n\\]\n\nThus, there are \\(\\boxed{2}\\) different complementary three-card sets.",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[2, '2']",
    "gold_answer": "Case 1: All three attributes are the same. This is impossible since sets contain distinct cards.\nCase 2: Two of the three attributes are the same. There are ${3\\choose 2}$ ways to pick the two attributes in question. Then there are $3$ ways to pick the value of the first attribute, $3$ ways to pick the value of the second attribute, and $1$ way to arrange the positions of the third attribute, giving us ${3\\choose 2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 = 27$ ways.\nCase 3: One of the three attributes are the same. There are ${3\\choose 1}$ ways to pick the one attribute in question, and then $3$ ways to pick the value of that attribute. Then there are $3!$ ways to arrange the positions of the next two attributes, giving us ${3\\choose 1} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3! = 54$ ways.\nCase 4: None of the three attributes are the same. We fix the order of the first attribute, and then there are $3!$ ways to pick the ordering of the second attribute and $3!$ ways to pick the ordering of the third attribute. This gives us $(3!)^2 = 36$ ways.\nAdding the cases up, we get $27 + 54 + 36 = \\boxed{117}$.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
